<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <!--
    The harness shipped inside this app embeds a JavaScript runtime, and the
    hardened runtime that signing applies forbids allocating executable memory
    unless it is asked for. Without these two the sidecar signs, verifies, and
    then dies on its first JIT allocation:

        Ran out of executable memory while allocating 128 bytes.

    Nothing about that says "entitlement", which is why it is written down here
    rather than left to be rediscovered. The webview needs JIT for the same
    reason, so this is what a Tauri app on macOS wants regardless.

    `allow-jit` alone is enough — checked by signing the harness with only this
    key and running it. `allow-unsigned-executable-memory` is broader and was
    not needed, so it is not here.
  -->
  <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit</key>
  <true/>
  <!--
    The personal office's server runs on the bundled Node and loads native
    modules out of the payload under Resources: libSQL, sharp, msgpackr.
    Under the hardened runtime, library validation lets a process load only
    code signed by Apple or by its own team, and those modules arrive signed
    by their publishers or by nobody. Without this key the server dies on
    its first `dlopen` with

        mapping process and mapped file (non-platform) have different Team IDs

    scripts/sign-payload.mjs re-signs the payload with the app's identity
    before bundling, which is what notarization requires anyway and what
    makes a release pass validation with or without this key. The key stays
    so that an ad-hoc development bundle — where nothing has a team — runs
    too. Applied to every binary Tauri signs; only `quintal-node` needs it.
  -->
  <key>com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation</key>
  <true/>
  <!--
    The hardened runtime that signing applies forbids audio input unless the
    app asks for it. Together with NSMicrophoneUsageDescription in Info.plist
    this is what lets the webview's getUserMedia reach the microphone; the
    webview's own capture-permission request is granted by wry.
  -->
  <key>com.apple.security.device.audio-input</key>
  <true/>
</dict>
</plist>
